{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Her have you proven for your servant, for Isaac.</b> Rashi explained: she is fitting for him, for she performs deeds of kindness. This passage is juxtaposed to the passage of Ephron the Hittite, for he too was of the seed of Canaan, as it says, ״וּכְנַעַן יָלַד אֶת צִידֹן בְּכֹרוֹ וְאֶת חֵת״ (Genesis 10:15), and Ephron was evil-eyed. Therefore [Abraham] commanded to distance the Canaanites, for they are possessors of the evil eye — for silver testifies and bears witness about everything, and every man is recognized by his pocket, whether his dealings are pure and straight. And there is no crucible and refining furnace for testing a man like silver and gold; and so Solomon said (Proverbs 17:3), ״מַצְרֵף לַכֶּסֶף וְכוּר לַזָּהָב״ — for one who wishes to refine a man and test him should go to the silver and see what its quality is, and so one who wishes a furnace to examine a man should go to the gold and see how the man conducts himself with it. For if he stands in his righteousness in monetary dealings, in every matter of wisdom he will be revealed as whole, and so too the opposite. From this Eliezer learned to test Rebecca only by this measure: whether she has a good eye and performs deeds of kindness. Therefore he said: I will demand of her nothing except that she give me to drink, and if she replies, ״שְׁתֵה וְגַם גְּמַלֶּיךָ אַשְׁקֶה״ (Genesis 24:14), then she is surely possessed of the trait of kindness, for she gives me more than what I ask. And if so, ״אֹתָהּ הוֹכַחְתָּ לְעַבְדְּךָ לְיִצְחָק״ — for his entire household is clothed in garments of salvation for humankind." "And by way of allusion he mentioned ״גְּמַלֶּיךָ״ — the language of גְּמִילוּת חֶסֶד is close to the language of ״גְּמַלֶּיךָ״. And so our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Tanḥuma Shemini 8): ״אֶת הַגָּמָל״ — this is Babylon, as it says (Psalms 137:8), ״אֶת גְּמוּלֵךְ שֶׁגָּמַלְתְּ לָנוּ״. And by this he meant: if she adds to say ״וְגַם גְּמַלֶּיךָ אַשְׁקֶה״, it is a sign of the matter — that she so loves the trait of kindness that she even loves everything called by the name of this trait, for this is a natural thing. And our Sages of blessed memory interpreted (Ḥullin 63a): ״אֶת הַחֲסִידָה״ — because it does kindness with its companions; therefore it is not far-fetched to explain also the word ״גָּמָל״, for perhaps its nature is to bestow kindness, one to another; therefore she adds love for them in saying ״וְגַם גְּמַלֶּיךָ אַשְׁקֶה״, for all this is a sign that she loves the trait of kindness. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Ta'anit 24a): ״כָּל כַּלָּה שֶׁעֵינֶיהָ יָפוֹת, כָּל גּוּפָהּ אֵין צְרִיכָה בְּדִיקָה״ — any bride whose eyes are beautiful, her whole body needs no examination. And this is something the senses contradict, for there are many ugly women in the world whose eyes are beautiful. And furthermore it is difficult: what did our Sages of blessed memory see to give advice to examine after this external beauty, when indeed ״שֶׁקֶר הַחֵן וְהֶבֶל הַיֹּפִי״ (Proverbs 31:30)? Rather, they surely gave advice to examine after her deeds, and this is the recommended counsel: that one should examine whether she is possessed of a beautiful eye and performs deeds of kindness — for if her eyes are beautiful toward people, then she is without doubt whole in all traits. And our Sages of blessed memory learned this from Eliezer, who did not test Rebecca except by this measure, for this measure is a paradigm for all the traits." "And according to its plain sense, it appears that Eliezer took this sign for himself, to say: I will ask of her only ״הַטִּי נָא כַדֵּךְ וְאֶשְׁתֶּה״ (Genesis 24:14), and I will not mention the camels to her nor request concerning them; and if she answers her words and says ״שְׁתֵה וְגַם גְּמַלֶּיךָ אַשְׁקֶה״ (Genesis 24:14) and shows concern for the camels more than for me, then surely the spirit of Elohim spoke through her — that the camels will be hers when Isaac marries her, and therefore she shows concern for the camels more than for me, for they are not mine and in the end they will be hers, and she is concerned for them because they will be her property. And what it says, ״מַחֲרִישׁ לָדַעַת הַהִצְלִיחַ ד׳ דַּרְכּוֹ אִם לֹא״ (Genesis 24:21) — for there are people who promise and do not deliver and boast with a false gift (Proverbs 25:14); therefore he was not confident in this sign until they finished drinking — then he relied on the sign and gave her the nose-ring and the bracelets."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:57.321548130Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}